[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  tuesday june 21st i'm zoe thomas for the  wall street journal  [Music]  short form videos snippets of advice  dancing pranks that run no more than a  few minutes and often just a few seconds  have become the latest big battleground  in social media  tiktok popularized short form videos and  it's been the front runner for some time  but as we told you last week it's facing  increased competition from rival service  google's youtube shorts  the company says shorts is now reaching  more than one and a half billion users a  month  so how did shorts which launched in 2020  get to this point so quickly  how do creators feel about using it and  what is youtube's end game in growing  this part of its platform  joining us to discuss all of this is our  social media reporter salvador rodriguez  hi sal thanks for being here hey zoe  thanks for having me  so start us off with a bit of background  why did youtube decide to launch shorts  in the first place and i mentioned it  was you know gaining on tick tock's  popularity but just how popular is it  yeah from what we can tell youtube  shorts might be second place in this  new type of social media content you  know in the us  we talked with the folks over at inmar  intelligence and they found that youtube  shorts is picked as the number two  preferred service for this type of short  form video and that's only gained since  the last time that they did this survey  back in september  for youtube they decided to go in on  this type of content because it just  made a ton of sense for them they're the  original  video platform of the internet right but  also you know i spoke with a lot of  creators and some of them pointed out  that in many ways tick tock poses a  threat to youtube youtube decided to  launch its shorts in india why did it  pick that market and what's it been like  for shorts growing there  yeah i think the reason they started in  india is because just before youtube  launched there in september 2020 a few  months prior india had actually banned  tik tok and several other chinese apps  they cited national security concerns as  the reason for doing that and that just  created a huge opening for youtube to go  in to a really huge market and you know  get started test out this new type of  thing but really gain a foothold in that  market and that's something that from  our reporting we can tell is continuing  to carry them today you know we spoke  with one creator his name is jonathan  lyons he uh he dresses up as a as a huge  rat and he posts these videos on youtube  shorts he told us that almost 80 percent  of his views are coming from india  and even more of his views come from  nearby country so it's really become a  stronghold for youtube  now as they grow and they've hit this  huge mark of 1.5 billion users you know  that's something that you can really  build upon and what they're really going  to have to do is make sure that they  build up that audience here in the us  because  they're doing good with india they're  doing good with older americans but when  it comes to advertising which is really  the next step the most desirable  audience for advertisers are those young  americans those young consumers that  they can you know grab a hold of and  keep us customers for a lifetime i want  to pick up on something you said you  know youtube shorts is popular with  older americans i mean why would  americans kind of 45 and up be  gravitating towards youtube shorts  youtube already had more than two  billion people who go to youtube  regularly  and they've also built out a ton of  analytics tools for creators so they can  get a sense of how well their videos are  doing um who's watching them and from  where and basically youtube took those  two things  and turned them into success you know  they've been around since 2005 so they  have older users at this point  and i think  one reason why if you're an older  american  and you're going to youtube shorts  instead of tick tick-tock it simply  comes down to  changing your habits you know you've  probably been going to youtube for more  than a decade and getting into tik-tok  it's not really hard but it still  requires that someone download one more  app sign up for one more account but i  guess the big question is how does  youtube transition you know from the  older viewers and from a big indian  market to a global market with younger  viewers  yeah i think it's gonna take time but  i've also noticed you know as a regular  consumer watching tv i've noticed that  they've been you know doing more and  more marketing just the other day i saw  youtube ran an ad for pride month and  they were saying like hey you know it's  pride month like show your pride by  posting a youtube short and every  every video that you post you know will  make one more donation to you know some  group involved in pride and i think like  those type of strategies will just pump  more and more content into that youtube  shorts pipeline and what they really  need to get to is to a place where their  videos are the ones that are starting  trends and really you know hitting that  cultural cachet because at this point in  time that's a big reason that tick tock  leads in this market  you've spoken to a bunch of creators who  are using youtube shorts and what have  they said about the experience of using  it  yeah well the creators they love the  analytics that they get with youtube  short so they can go in there and they  can see how old are my users what  geography are they coming from are they  on android are they on iphone they can  you know really get into the nitty  gritty of what's going on and that helps  them figure out you know the formulas  for  which videos are going to work which  videos are going to get me views and  another thing that folks love about  youtube shorts is that it just has  another one of those classic google  strengths which is that it's way easier  to search for a video on youtube shorts  than it is to search for a video on tick  tock or instagram reels the biggest  criticism though that we heard so far  from creators about youtube shorts is  that  when it comes to actually applying  special effects to videos  that is where youtube shorts is lacking  most i mean that's from the creator side  from from youtube side from google side  this has to be about you know ad revenue  right i mean how profitable are youtube  shorts for the company  yeah well right now it's not  particularly profitable just because  they haven't prioritized advertising  just yet  it wasn't until last month that they  finally rolled out ads globally for  youtube shorts but even now it's still  you know experimental and they're only  starting to ramp it up but certainly  that's going to be the direction that  they start heading in and they really do  need to get that going just because you  know  more recently their revenue growth has  been flagging as of late  all right that was our reporter salvador  rodriguez thanks for speaking with us  sal yeah thanks for having me  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website  wsj.com and if you like our show please  rate and review it you can do that  wherever you get your podcasts i'm zoe  thomas for the wall street journal  thanks for listening  [Music]

